            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest notable Perseverance rover news from Bing-grounded results is:\n\n- **Perseverance has driven a “marathon” on Mars.** NASA reported that the rover reached **26.2 miles / 42.195 km** of total driving on the Red Planet on **Sol 1,890**, after about **five years and four months** of surface operations. That is much faster than Opportunity, which took over 11 years to reach the same distance milestone【5:0†source】.\n\n- **A new orbital image shows the rover and its tracks.** NASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter, using the HiRISE camera, captured Perseverance from above on **June 13, 2026**, just before the marathon-distance milestone. The rover was operating west of Jezero Crater in an area nicknamed **“Arbot”**【5:0†source】.\n\n- **NASA also recently highlighted Perseverance science at Jezero’s rim.** NASA’s Mars news page lists recent Perseverance items, including a sweeping view of **Broom Point** and reporting that Perseverance found evidence that a **245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick** stack of ancient rock on Jezero Crater’s rim was formed by repeated asteroid impacts【5:4†source】.\n\n- **Fresh rover imagery is still being released.** NASA’s Perseverance image archive shows recent “Image of the Week” entries, including Week 284 for **July 19–25, 2026**, indicating the rover continues returning new visual data【5:2†source】.",
